00:00:15.560 --> 00:00:18.170
Starting on Google Cloud can
be overwhelming at first.

00:00:18.170 --> 00:00:20.366
There are over 40
products spread

00:00:20.366 --> 00:00:22.490
over a huge set of cloud
technologies, like machine

00:00:22.490 --> 00:00:25.340
learning, containers,
or big data.

00:00:25.340 --> 00:00:27.200
It could be hard to
decide where to dig in.

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:30.039
In my talk, I took people
on a journey of someone

00:00:30.039 --> 00:00:31.580
bringing their first
test application

00:00:31.580 --> 00:00:33.030
to Google Cloud Platform.

00:00:33.030 --> 00:00:35.030
Let's see if we can shed
some light on this path

00:00:35.030 --> 00:00:37.400
and help you figure out where
you want to go with GCP.

00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:40.220
One of the first things anybody
coming to any cloud does

00:00:40.220 --> 00:00:42.129
is what we call
a Lift and Shift.

00:00:42.129 --> 00:00:43.670
That's when you pick
up an app that's

00:00:43.670 --> 00:00:45.350
running in your
current environment

00:00:45.350 --> 00:00:46.870
and drop it to another.

00:00:46.870 --> 00:00:48.620
Now, most people
start this process

00:00:48.620 --> 00:00:50.900
from existing systems
running on either bare metal

00:00:50.900 --> 00:00:52.790
host or virtual
machines of their own,

00:00:52.790 --> 00:00:54.860
usually in their
own data centers.

00:00:54.860 --> 00:00:57.050
On the Cloud side of this
move, virtual machines

00:00:57.050 --> 00:00:59.990
are really the basic building
block of the solution.

00:00:59.990 --> 00:01:03.140
And on Google Cloud Platform,
that means Compute Engine.

00:01:03.140 --> 00:01:04.640
Now, I'm going to
attempt to explain

00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:06.089
all the benefits
of Compute Engine

00:01:06.089 --> 00:01:08.630
in the time it takes me to get
one of our virtual machines up

00:01:08.630 --> 00:01:09.630
and running.

00:01:09.630 --> 00:01:12.650
But first, let's jump into
the console to create it.

00:01:12.650 --> 00:01:14.360
You see here, I have
options around which

00:01:14.360 --> 00:01:16.775
of our worldwide data centers
you can host your VMs.

00:01:16.775 --> 00:01:20.360
I can change the machine
size, or the boot disk's

00:01:20.360 --> 00:01:22.190
basic firewall options,
and even choose

00:01:22.190 --> 00:01:25.010
to launch a container image by
itself in a virtual machine.

00:01:25.010 --> 00:01:28.307
I'm going to largely choose
the defaults here and launch.

00:01:28.307 --> 00:01:29.890
Then, I'm going to
take a deep breath.

00:01:29.890 --> 00:01:30.950
And then, I'm going
to try and spell out

00:01:30.950 --> 00:01:32.780
all the benefits
of Compute Engine

00:01:32.780 --> 00:01:34.370
before it finishes launching.

00:01:37.970 --> 00:01:40.292
Compute Engine instances
are highly configurable VMs

00:01:40.292 --> 00:01:42.250
that allow you to choose
both preset and custom

00:01:42.250 --> 00:01:44.416
amounts of processor memory
and processor platforms.

00:01:44.416 --> 00:01:46.270
You have several storage
options, including

00:01:46.270 --> 00:01:48.460
networked SSD and attached SSD.

00:01:48.460 --> 00:01:51.100
Use any of our disk images based
on several flavors of Linux

00:01:51.100 --> 00:01:54.320
or Windows to start or bring
your own customized image.

00:01:54.320 --> 00:01:54.820
You

00:01:54.820 --> 00:01:57.250
Can also take advantage of
some more specialized computing

00:01:57.250 --> 00:01:58.801
resources, like GPUs and TPUs.

00:01:58.801 --> 00:02:00.550
You can manage all of
your VMs, regardless

00:02:00.550 --> 00:02:02.350
of where in the world they
are, on one screen and one

00:02:02.350 --> 00:02:02.890
interface.

00:02:02.890 --> 00:02:06.280
And this just isn't a feature of
the console for managing them.

00:02:06.280 --> 00:02:07.931
All of your VMs are
on the same network

00:02:07.931 --> 00:02:09.639
by default, with no
manual setup required

00:02:09.639 --> 00:02:12.130
to get your VM in Sydney to
talk to your VM in London.

00:02:12.130 --> 00:02:13.872
And we're done.

00:02:13.872 --> 00:02:16.330
And you can tell we're done
because we have this little SSH

00:02:16.330 --> 00:02:16.830
button.

00:02:16.830 --> 00:02:20.500
So I couldn't get it done in
the time it took to launch.

00:02:20.500 --> 00:02:21.510
But let's go ahead.

00:02:21.510 --> 00:02:22.540
We'll SSH in.

00:02:22.540 --> 00:02:24.816
So now, we'll use that to
correct right to the VM

00:02:24.816 --> 00:02:25.690
through the browser--

00:02:25.690 --> 00:02:28.810
no pulling down and
customizing certs, setting up

00:02:28.810 --> 00:02:30.310
all the necessary security.

00:02:30.310 --> 00:02:31.660
We'll do that for you.

00:02:31.660 --> 00:02:33.850
From here, I can
customize my VM,

00:02:33.850 --> 00:02:37.150
installing whatever software I
need to start making it mine.

00:02:41.038 --> 00:02:42.982
[KEYBOARD CLICKING]

00:02:50.758 --> 00:02:54.410
But I don't have to SSH directly
into the box to do that.

00:02:54.410 --> 00:02:56.600
With the G Cloud
CLI tools, I can

00:02:56.600 --> 00:02:59.390
do everything I need to do
to make this machine mine

00:02:59.390 --> 00:03:01.670
right from the comfort of
my own terminal window.

00:03:01.670 --> 00:03:04.310
Pure Lift and Shift really only
requires a virtual machine.

00:03:04.310 --> 00:03:06.200
But moving to the
Cloud allows you

00:03:06.200 --> 00:03:07.820
to take advantage
of a lot of systems

00:03:07.820 --> 00:03:10.195
that you can't duplicate in
your own environment-- things

00:03:10.195 --> 00:03:11.810
like distributed
storage, distributed

00:03:11.810 --> 00:03:15.550
databases, or machine learning
APIs and big data tools.

00:03:15.550 --> 00:03:17.330
In the full video,
I spoke about how

00:03:17.330 --> 00:03:20.060
best to take an application you
brought to the Cloud via Lift

00:03:20.060 --> 00:03:23.270
and Shift and re-architect it
to take advantage of everything

00:03:23.270 --> 00:03:25.110
that Google Cloud has to offer.

00:03:25.110 --> 00:03:27.020
I go over using Cloud
storage and Cloud SQL

00:03:27.020 --> 00:03:29.069
to make your app
much more scalable.

00:03:29.069 --> 00:03:30.860
I also explored some
of the other computing

00:03:30.860 --> 00:03:33.890
options that open up to you
when you make those architecture

00:03:33.890 --> 00:03:35.250
changes.

00:03:35.250 --> 00:03:37.566
Finally, I'll point you
to a number of other tools

00:03:37.566 --> 00:03:40.190
that you can use to improve your
application, including machine

00:03:40.190 --> 00:03:43.345
learning APIs, and logging,
and other communication tools.

